{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` recreates the `compose-review-bot-1` container whenever `main` advances\nand the bot's source changed. It does NOT drain in-flight reviews first, so recreation stops uvicorn and\nKILLS whatever code review is running. Because a full code review takes far longer than the gap between\ndeploys during a landing burst, reviews can be killed before they ever vote — and the gate LIVE-LOCKS:\nno change gets an `LLM-Review` vote, so nothing can submit, which is indistinguishable from an outage.\n\n## MEASURED, live, on 2026-08-03 (account 896586841071, instance i-00880b2c7f13527c5)\n\nSEVEN recreations in 90 minutes, from `journalctl -u rebar-autodeploy`:\n    16:23:05, 16:41:17, 16:48:22, 17:10:06, 17:14:46, 17:29:50, 17:49:28\n    => gaps of 18, 7, 22, 4, 15, 20 minutes\n\nA full code review, from the bot's own logs, is SEVEN passes and takes ~10 MINUTES:\n    code-review-base 94.6s / 103.5s / 134.9s · code-review-tests 92.3s / 125.3s\n    code-review-scope-intent 29.1s / 101.0s · code-review-api-compat 96.7s / 114.1s\n    code-review-verify 80.9s / 137.6s · plus deletion-impact, phase-boundaries, coach\n    >1,000,000 input tokens per change (LLM_TOKEN_USAGE: input 1,010,039, cache_read 883,980)\n\nFOUR of the six gaps are SHORTER than one review. The interruption was observed directly in the log —\npasses running, then `INFO: Shutting down` / `Application shutdown complete` / `Finished server\nprocess [1]`, mid-review.\n\nUSER-VISIBLE IMPACT observed at the time: changes 1302 (`Verified +1` at 17:24) and 1303 (`Verified +1`\nat 17:32) both sat with `LLM-Review = 0` for 20-35 minutes, unsubmittable.\n\n## Why NO ALARM FIRED — this is the part that matters\n\nThe bot is HEALTHY by every signal the monitoring has:\n  - `docker inspect`: `running`, `restarts=0`\n  - every deploy logged `{\"event\":\"autodeploy\",\"msg\":\"review-bot redeployed + healthy\"}`\n  - ZERO `VOTER_ERROR` lines, so `rebar-gerrit-voter-errors` (monitoring_s4b.tf) stayed `OK`\n  - all 11 CloudWatch alarms `OK` throughout\nA killed review emits NO `VOTER_ERROR`, because nothing failed — the process was asked to stop. So the\nfail-closed voter path never runs and the alarm that eb6e's rollback plan depends on cannot see this\nfailure mode AT ALL. **The gate can be fully live-locked while every health signal reads green.**\n\n## Why this is not self-evidently benign\n\nInterrupted reviews ARE retried by the reconciler, so the system self-heals ONCE deploys stop. The defect\nis that it cannot converge while deploys keep arriving faster than reviews complete — and a landing burst\nis exactly when the gate is most needed. Convergence depends on the burst ending, not on any bound in the\ndeploy path.\n\n## Relevance to the Bedrock cutover [rebar:eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a]\n\neb6e removes the cross-provider fallback and names the `VOTER_ERROR` CloudWatch alarm as its safety net.\nThis bug shows a live-lock the gate can enter that the alarm provably does not observe, so that safety net\nhas a blind spot independent of provider. Worth resolving, or at least recording, before the cutover\nrelies on it.\n\n## Approach\n\nMake recreation wait for in-flight work, rather than making reviews survive recreation. Options to weigh:\n  (a) DRAIN: before `docker compose up -d`, poll a bot endpoint for in-flight review count and wait up to\n      a bounded timeout; recreate immediately when idle. Needs an endpoint exposing that count.\n  (b) DEFER: if the bot reports busy, skip this deploy cycle and retry on the next timer tick (~2 min).\n      Simplest, and autodeploy is already an idempotent oneshot timer, so skipping is natural.\n  (c) COALESCE: debounce — require N seconds of no new `main` commits before deploying, so a burst\n      produces one deploy instead of seven.\n(b) and (c) compose well and need no new bot surface. Whatever is chosen, bound the wait so a permanently\nbusy bot cannot block deploys forever, and LOG the deferral so a skipped deploy is visible rather than\nsilent.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A deploy that would interrupt an in-flight review is DEFERRED or DRAINED rather than recreating the\n      container mid-review, proven by a test or a scripted simulation of the busy case.\n- [ ] The deferral/drain is BOUNDED, so a permanently-busy bot cannot block deploys indefinitely, and the\n      bound is stated with its rationale.\n- [ ] A deferred deploy is LOGGED and visible (journald + the autodeploy metric), so a skipped cycle can\n      never be mistaken for no change having landed.\n- [ ] The interrupted-review failure mode becomes OBSERVABLE: either it emits a countable marker that an\n      alarm can watch, or the gap is explicitly recorded as accepted with its reasoning — the current\n      state, where a fully live-locked gate leaves every health signal green, is what this criterion\n      exists to end.\n- [ ] A landing BURST is covered, not just a single deploy: a simulation with commits arriving faster than\n      one review completes ends with the review voting rather than being killed repeatedly.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass by exit code; the default selection is green.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785779726364470001,"uuid":"8e71043d-9c10-495a-9ed9-13601ba02a25"}